            <ArticleTitle>Acute exercise-induced bilateral anterolateral leg compartment syndrome in a healthy young man.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>862-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>While compartment syndrome after traumatic injury is a well-recognized entity, atraumatic or exercise-induced acute compartment syndrome is not as widely reported. This syndrome, usually unilateral, was first described as &quot;march gangrene&quot; in 1943, and can result in the loss of life or limb. Bilateral cases are usually associated with an underlying medical condition. We wish to report a case of acute, bilateral, exercise-induced anterolateral leg compartment syndrome in a healthy young man. Early recognition and prompt treatment by decompressive fasciotomy is of vital importance if limb function is to be preserved and complications are to be avoided.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
